{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head 5ff918e80e77e74972aab0bba483c02aa2fd55d7 on request/merge-succession-reviewed-paths, branched from main 04c4a267, pushed and clean. Primary artifact 6a438f38 at cmd/gs, with live candidates 38716d9e at internal/workroom and 5416da17 at docs; all three effective, live, non-stale, naming that exact head. This answers your request 86c09996. My ready-for-review report is df16e184 and carries the condition-by-condition detail.\n\nThe reproduce, first, because it is the whole case. Replaying the live projection through the bound as it stood, your checkpoint approval 5c16df96 for head 7a9e94db reached exactly [internal/kernel] and refused internal/app, cmd/gs and docs. After the repair it reaches all four. I did not run gs merge against that head and retired none of its predecessors.\n\nThe bound, and the judgement I want from you. Reach is now every path where the implementer stood an artifact at the exact head the verdict names, already recorded when the reviewer signed. Your guidance offered the candidate set keyed on head and implementer; I added the third clause, that the artifact must predate the approval, because without it the set is mintable — an implementer could publish a candidate at any path after the verdict and reach it, which is the round-two forgery finding wearing a real commit. With it, the reachable set is fixed at the moment the reviewer signed. All four checkpoint candidates stand at sequences 5184 to 5187 against the approval at 5195, so the real case satisfies it.\n\nWhat I did not do, deliberately. gs review still cites one artifact. Teaching it to cite the whole set is the strictly narrower and more truthful contract, because then the reach would be reviewer-written rather than implementer-written, and I think it is worth doing — but it changes what a verdict carries and what every future approval must contain, which is your architecture call, not something to slip into a repair. The merge page states the current limit in those terms: holding no repository the fold takes the implementer word that the head touches each path, a public claim checkable against Git but not by the fold. Tell me if you want that change now and under which request.\n\nThe circular basis needed a second fix you may want to look at closely. Work stands on what it replaces, so a successor cites the predecessor it grew out of, and the same merge withdraws that predecessor; the successor went stale at birth and gs merge refuses a stale successor, so the merge refused its own result. A record now carries the signed plan of any receipt it rests on. Only the signed plan is carried, so nothing is granted that the merge did not already declare, and a retirement outside the plan or any other basis still propagates — TestMergeReceiptDoesNotHideLaterApprovalRetirement still holds.\n\nThe command mirrors the fold exactly, on all three facts. A preflight looser than the fold strands a succession after HEAD has moved; stricter, and it refuses merges the fold would allow, which is how the checkpoint got stuck. If you find those two readings disagreeing anywhere, that is the defect to report.\n\nGates: go vet and go build clean, go test ./... -count=1 fully green, 23 packages together and internal/docset alone at 84s. flock is absent on this machine so the docset run was serialized by a ps check rather than the lock file — worth knowing if you gate on that file. The resident understands state@1: bin/gs built 14:24 carries the constant and main 04c4a267 sets SchemaState to it.\n\nDo not merge this head, and do not merge the checkpoint head or retire its predecessors as part of any verdict here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6a438f389b220fc8e73c051bc332a731c76dfb48","conditions":"Architecture, Security and Simplification review of exact head 5ff918e8. A merge receipt reaches the lineages of every path where the implementer stood an artifact at the exact approved head before the reviewer signed, so the checkpoint approval reaches all four trees it reviewed instead of one; a record carries the signed plan of any receipt it rests on, so a successor may cite the predecessor the same merge retires without going stale; the command applies the same reading before Git is touched, and a refusal leaves target HEAD, receipt reservation and durable log unchanged. Every earlier refusal stays green and each new bound is mutation-proved in both directions. Verdict on the exact head; do not merge, and do not merge the checkpoint head or retire its predecessors.","head":"5ff918e80e77e74972aab0bba483c02aa2fd55d7","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}